  "dependency": "DUB-17 Theorem 2.6 / DUB-18 Corollary 8.2 -> higher symmetric-power continuation -> residual modular Galois association -> integral-to-residual H1 eigenclass -> finite integral H1 and its actual Hecke-compatible coefficient change.",
  "discharged": "For every original normalized primitive form and every source weight k >= 2, the actual column-binary native H1 class is nonzero and is an eigenvector of every full good-prime Hecke operator with its original Fourier eigenvalue. The original dual-period comparison is explicitly the diagonal of signed binomial coefficients and respects the literal adjugate diagonal. On every cocycle the transported operator is exactly the sum over the original Gamma0 cosets with the column diag(p,1) action. The full H1 operator has that actual cocycle representative; no eigenclass, nonvanishing, coefficient identification, Hecke compatibility or quotient-descent premise is supplied. At every actual source level and weight, integral column-binary native H1 is a finite Z-module, with Gamma0 finite generation derived using native Schreier and modular-group generation and coefficient finiteness from the existing monomial basis. The actual polynomial coefficient map descends to full native H1 and commutes with the full original Hecke correspondence over every coefficient ring. Its complex specialization equals the already proved analytic-period operator, and the original primitive form gives its nonzero eigenclass with the original good-prime Fourier eigenvalues. No finiteness, quotient-descent, Hecke comparison or eigenclass premise is supplied in these original-object consumers.",
  "boundary": "Spanning by integral cocycles, the integral Hecke character, full Hecke commutativity and nonzero residual eigensystem reduction still require proof. The coefficient-change map alone does not assert injectivity or preservation of nonzero classes. Finite-characteristic weight/level reduction, geometric Galois association, an all-weight adic system, purity, crystallinity, Sato-Tate and higher symmetric-power automorphy remain open. Acceptance remains 16/20; DUB-17--20 are open.",
